Knowledge Check
Answer in your own words:

What is the difference between a class and an object?
-- A class describes an object with fields and gives functionality to an object.
-- An object is an instance of the class, making the class able to implement the functionalities.

What is a constructor?
-- A constructor is a special method with the same name as the class and no return type, used to initialize a new object when the new keyword is called.

What does this refer to?
--  This calls a variable in a class into the constructor, so that it can be used a parameter in the object.

What is encapsulation?
-- Encapsulation is on of the 4 pillars of OOP, essentially restricting data within a child class so that it can be accessed by the parent class.
--  using private fields and public getter and setter methods

What does extends do?
-- Extend allows child classes to use methods in parent classes

What is method overriding?
-- It is when a child class is intended to override a method from its parent class

Explain polymorphism using the animal example.
-- Polymorphism means "many forms", and it occurs when we have many classes that are related to each other by inheritance.
-- For example, think of a superclass called Animal that has a method called animalSound().
-- Subclasses of Animals could be Pigs, Cats, Dogs, Birds - And they also have their own implementation of an animal sound

What is an abstract class?
-- An abstract class is a restricted class that cannot be used to create objects

Why might a field be private?
-- You only want it to be accessible within the declared class

